<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Banzuke Surfing Game</title>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
    <!-- Link to the external CSS file -->
    <!-- Uncomment this if you have styles to include -->
    <!-- <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"> -->
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to Banzuke Surfing Game!</h1>
    <p id="user"></p>
    <p>Select your Rikishi and start playing!</p>
    <select id="rikishi">
        <option value="1">Rikishi 1</option>
        <option value="2">Rikishi 2</option>
        <!-- more options here -->
    </select>
    <button id="startPlayingButton">Start Playing</button>
    <hr>
    <h2>Admin Panel</h2>
    <p>Switch user:</p>
    <input id="userSwitch" type="text">
    <button id="switchUserButton">Switch User</button>
    <p>Backfill contest results:</p>
    <input id="backfillContest" type="text" placeholder="Enter contest name">
    <input id="backfillRikishi" type="text" placeholder="Enter rikishi selection">
    <button id="backfillResultsButton">Backfill Results</button>
    <!-- Link to the external JavaScript file -->
    <script type="module" src="game.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

Note the following changes:

I have removed the onclick attributes from your buttons. Instead, the
event listeners are being added inside game.js, which should provide
better separation of concerns and allow your JavaScript to handle its
own logic.

In the <script> tag linking to game.js, I added the type="module"
attribute. This tells the browser to treat this script as an ES6 module,
which allows import and export statements.

Remember to check that your server is configured to serve .js files with
the correct MIME type (application/javascript), as some servers do not
do this by default for files imported as modules.

